<edge source="china_ai_chip_rivalry::export_control_prevalenc" target="global_bond_market_shock::cross_border_capital_flo" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Rapid expansion of export controls legally blocks shipments, forces cancellation or delay of cross-border contracts and FDI approvals, and prompts multinationals to halt or repatriate investment, reducing net cross-border capital flows.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_ai_chip_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_market_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_ai_chip_rivalry::export_control_prevalenc" target="global_bond_market_shock::long_term_bond_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Broadening and institutionalization of export controls increases geopolitical fragmentation and the term premium investors demand for sovereign risk, exerting upward pressure on long-dated government bond yields.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_ai_chip_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_market_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_ai_chip_rivalry::export_control_prevalenc" target="global_oil_market_shift::sanctions_enforcement" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Rapid escalation and formalization of export controls on AI chips prompts allied governments to extend those controls into coordinated sanctioning regimes and stricter enforcement against entities facilitating restricted tech transfers, increasing overall sanctions enforcement intensity for related trade channels.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_ai_chip_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_oil_market_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_ai_chip_rivalry::export_control_prevalenc" target="global_oil_market_shift::shipping_capacity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Broader export controls that cover navigation, automation, or dual‑use electronics used in tankers and port equipment disrupt parts supply, maintenance and certification cycles for vessels, causing temporary withdrawals, slower port turnarounds and a net reduction in effective tanker and tanker-support capacity.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_ai_chip_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_oil_market_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_ai_chip_rivalry::export_control_prevalenc" target="iran_regional_conflict::intel_info_flow_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">An increase in export controls leads to more inspections, seizures and reporting by customs and enforcement agencies, which generates and disseminates more actionable intelligence about procurement networks tied to Iran.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_ai_chip_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_ai_chip_rivalry::export_control_prevalenc" target="russia_ukraine_war::military_supply_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Expanded export controls on AI and dual‑use chips lead to more license denials and blocked shipments of components used in Russian drones, targeting pods and munitions production, directly reducing the volume and readiness of equipment delivered to frontline units.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">china_ai_chip_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
