<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_stringency" target="china_ai_chip_rivalry::export_control_prevalenc" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalation of sanctions regimes tied to the Russia–Ukraine war drives governments to design, adopt and operationalize broader dual‑use export‑control lists and licensing regimes that are then applied to advanced AI‑capable chips and related suppliers, increasing the prevalence of export controls targeting that technology.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">china_ai_chip_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_stringency" target="china_ai_chip_rivalry::investor_risk_perception" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">A visible rise in stringent sanctions and secondary‑sanctions practice around the Russia war increases investor awareness of sanction exposure and tail risk, leading investors to mark up perceived political and compliance risks for Chinese AI‑chip firms and suppliers.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">china_ai_chip_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_stringency" target="global_bond_market_shock::cross_border_capital_flo" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Tighter, broad financial sanctions increase counterparty and country risk perceptions, driving rapid reallocation of international portfolios into safe‑haven sovereign bonds and thereby increasing net cross‑border inflows into core bond markets.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_market_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_stringency" target="global_bond_market_shock::long_term_bond_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalating sanctions that restrict energy exports and payment channels drive up global commodity and inflation expectations, which lift inflation risk premia and therefore long‑term sovereign bond yields.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_market_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_stringency" target="global_oil_market_shift::sanctions_enforcement" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Raising the legal scope and specificity of sanctions prompts authorities to deploy additional investigative, customs and financial-control resources (asset freezes, ship detentions, designation of facilitators), directly increasing enforcement intensity against oil-related trade.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_oil_market_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_stringency" target="iran_regional_conflict::energy_supply_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">Tighter sanctions on Russian energy exports tighten global supply buffers and raise prices, prompting rerouting, heavier shipping volumes through chokepoints and competitive market pressures that make regional energy shipments more vulnerable to disruption from Iranian-linked attacks or interdictions.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_regional_conflict</data>
</edge>
